{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>You shall not learn to do according to the abominations of those nations</b>. This too is an explicit commandment, for He already said (Leviticus 18:3), \"According to the doings of the land of Canaan, to which I am bringing you, you shall not do, and in their statutes you shall not walk,\" and now He mentions their deeds and says that they are an abomination before HaShem. And He mentioned <b>one who passes his son or daughter through fire</b>, which is a species of sorcery according to the plain sense, or the Molech according to the opinion of our Sages (Sanhedrin 64a), and this is the truth, as I explained in its place (Leviticus 18:21). And He mentioned <b>a sorcerer (מְכַשֵּׁף)</b> as a general category for all sorceries, and <b>a charmer (חֹבֵר חָבֶר)</b> and <b>one who consults a familiar spirit (שֹׁאֵל אוֹב)</b> and <b>a wizard (וְיִדְּעֹנִי)</b>, and these are particulars within sorcery, and He forbade them also to the one who consults. And <b>one who consults the dead (דֹרֵשׁ אֶל הַמֵּתִים)</b> — this is like the <b>ov</b> itself, in any matter in which one might consult them. And He mentioned <b>a diviner of events (קוֹסֵם קְסָמִים)</b>, which is speculation about the future in general, from the language of <b>\"A divine sentence (קֶסֶם) is on the lips of the king; in judgment his mouth shall not transgress\"</b> (Proverbs 16:10), as it says (Joshua 13:22), <b>\"Balaam son of Beor, the diviner (הַקּוֹסֵם),\"</b> and he was also an augur, as it says of him (Numbers 24:1), <b>\"And he did not go as before, time after time, to meet auguries (נְחָשִׁים),\"</b> and it is written (I Samuel 28:8), <b>\"Divine for me (קָסֳמִי), I pray you, by a familiar spirit.\"</b> And He mentioned in particular <b>an observer of clouds (מְעוֹנֵן)</b>, which is one who knows by means of clouds, and <b>an augur (מְנַחֵשׁ)</b>, which is one who looks at birds — at their wings or at their chirping — as in the matter that is written (Ecclesiastes 10:20), <b>\"For a bird of the heavens shall carry the voice, and the winged creature shall tell the matter,\"</b> and it is a term derived from the language of <b>\"I made haste (חַשְׁתִּי) and did not delay\"</b> (Psalms 119:60), for whatever one hastens to make known about the future before it comes to pass is called <b>nachash</b>, that is to say <b>נָחִישׁ</b> (we hasten) and <b>נַקְדִּים</b> (we precede) to know.\n\nAnd Scripture says, <b>\"For an abomination to YHWH is everyone who does these\"</b>, and it did not say <b>\"everyone who does all of these\"</b>, for Scripture speaks of the majority of them, since the <b>observer of clouds</b> and the <b>augur</b> are not an abomination, and He did not dispossess the Canaanites on their account, for all human beings desire to know their future and engage in many such wisdoms, to their own minds. And now, know and understand regarding the matters of sorceries: the Creator, blessed be He, when He created everything from nothing, made the supernal beings the governors of the lower beings that are beneath them, and He gave the power of the earth and all that is upon it to the stars and the constellations, according to their governance and their gaze upon them, as is tested in the science of astrology. And He further made, over the stars and constellations, governing angels and princes that are soul to them. Now, their governance from the time of their creation until forever and ever is a decree of the supernal beings that He set for them. But among His mighty wonders, He placed within the power of the supernal governors paths of exchange and forces to exchange the governance of that which is beneath them: if the gaze of the stars — at the \"faces\" that are toward the earth — is for good or for evil to the land or to a nation or to an individual, the supernal \"faces\" can exchange it for him to the opposite in the gaze itself, as in the matter that they said: <b>the exchange of pleasure is affliction</b>. And He did this so that He, blessed be His name, who changes the seasons and the times, who calls to the waters of the sea to do with them as He wills, and who turns morning into deepest darkness, [does so] without changing the nature of the world and [while] letting the stars and constellations proceed in their ordered course.\n\nAnd therefore the author of the Book of the Moon, the wise scholar, said in <i>Nagromonsia</i>: when the Moon — which is called \"the sphere of the world\" — is at the head of Aries, by way of example, and its faces are opposite a certain person, you make an image for a certain purpose and inscribe upon it the name of the hour and the name of the angel appointed over it, from those names mentioned in that book, and you perform such-and-such an incense offering in such-and-such a manner, the gaze upon it will be for evil — to pluck up and to break down and to destroy and to overthrow. And when the Moon is in a certain constellation, you make an image and an incense offering in a certain manner for all good — to build and to plant. Now, this too is the governance of the Moon through the power of its governors, but the direct governance that proceeds in its course is the desire of the Creator, blessed be He, which He set in them from of old — whereas this is the opposite. And this is the secret of sorceries and their power, concerning which they said of them (Hullin 7a) that they <b>contradict the celestial entourage</b> — meaning that they are the opposite of the direct forces, and they are a contradiction to the entourage from one of the sides.\n\nAnd therefore it is fitting that the Torah forbid them, so that the world be left to its customary course and its simple nature, which is the desire of its Creator. And this is also among the reasons for the prohibition of mixed kinds (כִּלְאַיִם), for from the crossbreeding of plants there come strange effects, producing changes from the order of the world for evil or for good — apart from the fact that they themselves are a change in creation, as I have already explained (Leviticus 19:19).\n\nNow, many will take issue with the auguries, saying that there is no truth in them at all — for who can tell the raven and the starling what will be? But we cannot deny things that become publicly known before the eyes of witnesses, and our Sages also acknowledge them, as they said in Vayikra Rabbah (32:2) and Kohelet Rabbah (10:23): <b>\"For a bird of the heavens shall carry the voice\"</b> (Ecclesiastes 10:20) — this refers to the raven and the wisdom of the <i>tiyarin</i>; \"birds\" in Arabic is <i>ṭā'ir</i>, and the masters of birds are called <i>tiyarin</i>. And this matter is also mentioned in the Gemara (Gittin 45a). But there is a secret to this matter, and we have already informed (Leviticus 18:25) that the constellations have princes that govern them, and they are souls to the dwellings of the spheres, and the princes of the Tail and of Aries, which are close to the earth — these are the ones called \"the princes of the Thread\" — make known the future. And from them signs are made in the birds, through which they make known future events; but they do not announce for a long time, nor do they tell of distant futures — only regarding imminent events do they make known: some of them through the voice of the bird, when it calls out with a bitter cry concerning a dead person, and some of them through the spreading of its wings. And this is what it says: <b>\"shall carry the voice\"</b> — to those that tell by their voice; <b>\"the winged creature shall tell the matter\"</b> — to those that hint through their wings. And all of this is not considered an abomination among the nations, but rather is accounted as wisdom for them. And thus they said (Bamidbar Rabbah 19:3): <b>\"And Solomon's wisdom exceeded the wisdom of all the children of the east\"</b> (I Kings 5:10) — what was the wisdom of the children of the east? They were knowledgeable and subtle in <i>ṭayyar</i>. And likewise Scripture says (Isaiah 2:6), <b>\"For they are filled from the east and augurers (וְעֹנְנִים) like the Philistines.\"</b> Now, Solomon learned this among his wisdoms. And the knowledge is the understanding of the chirping, and the subtlety of inferring a matter from the spreading of the wings.\n\nAnd when Scripture included the <b>observers of clouds</b> and the <b>diviners</b> together with the abominations previously mentioned, it went back and specified (Deuteronomy 18:14): <b>\"These nations, which you are about to dispossess, listen to observers of clouds and diviners; but as for you, YHWH your Elohim has not given you so.\"</b> It says: behold, HaShem has forbidden you these previously mentioned practices because they are abominations before Him, and on their account He dispossessed those nations from before you; and He has forbidden you auguries and divinations because He has done a great elevation for you, to set you supreme above all the nations of the earth, in that He will establish a prophet in your midst and place His words in his mouth, and you shall hear from him what El will do — and you will not need, for the future, to turn to a diviner or an augur, who receive their knowledge from the stars or from the lower ones among the supernal princes, for none of their words are fully true, nor do they make known everything necessary. But prophecy makes known the desire of HaShem, and not a single thing shall fall from all its words — and this is what He specifies (Deuteronomy 18:22): <b>\"That thing which YHWH has not spoken.\"</b> Now, you are His portion and His treasure, hearing His counsel from His mouth, while they are the portion of the constellations, going after them. And this is the reason for <b>\"but as for you, YHWH your Elohim has not given you so\"</b> — He has not given to you as He gave to them, in the sense of <b>\"whom YHWH your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples\"</b> (Deuteronomy 4:19), as I explained (Leviticus 18:25).\n\nAnd in the Sifrei (Shofetim 174): <b>\"They listen to observers of clouds and diviners\"</b> — lest you say to them, \"They have something to ask, but I have nothing to ask,\" Scripture comes and says: <b>\"But as for you, YHWH your Elohim has not given you so.\"</b> Now, this is a proof for everything we have explained, for in the auguries a real root is found, and therefore Israel would have had a claim regarding the withholding of them from them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the author of ספר הלבנה (Book of the Moon) and names the work נגרמונסי\"א; this is transliterated as Nagromonsia without resolution, as the precise identification of this work is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:21.800690358Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}